{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"yin.k/requires|fixed point|manifest|address|hash|module\\\" docs/design/yin.vm.dependency-completion.md | head -80; rg -n \\\"load-vector|code-aliases|yin.code/hash\\\" src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc | head -50; sed -n '1,120p' src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"1:# yin.vm dependency completion — the work-item fixed point (U14 design)\n5:> and a traversal. Supplies the `:yin.k/requires`, `:yin.k/store`, and\n12:  specifies: `:yin.k/requires` (every field), the reachable store slice (§7.6.2),\n24:| address | a content address `:segment/sha256-…` (`dao.jing/segment-key`), of a segment vector or a tree root row |\n26:| work item | `[address context]`, the unit of analysis (§7.7.3) |\n27:| code facts | what one address contributes regardless of context (§5.1) |\n28:| value facts | what one context contributes regardless of address (§5.2) |\n30:| discharge | the ruling on one obligation: by store key, by primitive profile, by module export, or none |\n36:(nothing is ever removed; that is what makes the fixed point a fixed point):\n39:     ;; code, fetched once per address (§7.7.3 \"code once\")\n40:     :code        {address → {:kind   :segment | :tree\n44:     :items       #{[address context]}     ; analyzed\n45:     :frontier    queue of [address context]   ; discovered, not yet analyzed\n48:     :values      {:closures   #{[address entry context]}\n54:     :obligations {sym → :store | :primitive | :module | :undischarged}\n60:     :requires    {:yin.k/segments #{address}\n62:                   :yin.k/modules {name → manifest-address}\n68:     :missing     {:segments   #{address}        ; fetch failed → :blocked\n70:                   :footprints #{module-name}    ; module without declared footprint → :incomplete\n74:                    | :ffi-pair-key | :unaddressed-segment, …}]}\n79:  neither. This is the \"every newly discovered `[address context]` pair is analyzed,\n80:  whether or not the address was seen before\" rule.\n81:- `:code` is keyed by address, never by the VM's runtime segment id. A runtime\n82:  segment id is translated through the loaded image's `:address` (`semantic.cljc`\n83:  `load-vector` records it, `:code-aliases` inverts it). An image with no address is\n84:  refused (`:unaddressed-segment`): there is nothing portable to name.\n103:      closure              → [:closure address(segment) entry context(env)]\n108:                           → [:k address(segment) pc context(env)\n116:    abstract-frame({:type :return :segment :pc :env}) = [:frame address(segment) pc context(env)]\n119:  No content hash is required. (A `:yin.k.ctx/sha256-…` may be derived for\n127:  Together with the finite address set, `:items` is finite and the walk terminates.\n134:Analysis of `[address context]` factorizes:\n136:    facts([address context]) = code-facts(address) ∪ value-facts(context)\n139:already-walked address costs one map lookup for the code half. The work item\n142:### 5.1 code-facts(address)\n144:Computed once, when the address is first fetched.\n158:Fetch is through a caller-supplied `fetch : address → canonical-vector | {:root :rows} | nil`.\n161:\"resolves to no payload\" throw) records the address in `:missing :segments` and\n175:first `:return` at or after `body`. Emit a scope relation `$scope = #{[address pc c]}`\n189:Because segment instructions are positionally addressed (one pc, one row), the\n194:Precedence when both are available for one address: if the ledger (U9) holds a\n222:    (namespace sym) names a module the\n223:    module registry holds                → :module    ; :yin.k/modules gains {name manifest-address};\n229:modules. Env is skipped on purpose — UCF §7.6.1 *Names*.\n234:         profile-record = {:yin.k/profile address :yin.k/class :pure|:effectful|:host\n276:`[address(:segment) context(:env)]`, one work item per `:k` frame, and the\n286:    complete : {:vm :fetch :registry :modules :contract} → result\n288:    1. Refuse early: :ready-queue non-empty; any loaded image without :address.\n290:         [address(:control :segment) context(:env)]          ; the frame\n291:         [address(f :segment) context(f :env)] for f in :k   ; K frames\n318:Termination: §4 (finite contexts) × finite addresses; every fold is monotone and\n323:    {:yin.k/requires  (:requires state) with\n357:   `[address context]`.\n360:6. A segment whose address `fetch` cannot resolve → `:blocked`, the remainder\n375:4. **U13 interface — Modify (needs modules and alias tie-breaker):**\n376:   Incomplete. U14 consumes `profile-of` and `name-of`, but §6 also consumes a **module** registry lookup. U13 must own this (e.g., `module-of`) or §6 must name its real owner. Furthermore, `name-of` must have a deterministic tie-breaker (e.g., lexicographically least) for aliases to guarantee portability.\n75:   code-aliases]) ; {address segment-id}; additive, one address one id (UCF §7.3.4)\n535:   `:yin.code/hash` (§2.2) — a claim checked here: the canonical vector the\n611:          claimed (get-in attrs [seg :yin.code/hash])\n684:           :code-aliases (store-alias (:code-aliases vm) image)\n710:(defn load-vector\n725:  ([vm v] (load-vector vm v {}))\n732:         aliases (:code-aliases vm)\n743:            :code-aliases (store-alias aliases image)\n844:                                  :code-aliases {},\n(ns yin.vm.module\n  \"Module system for Yin VM v2.\n\n   The v1 module system kept two `defonce` atoms and mutated them at load\n   time. A registry is a value here: an ordinary map carried in VM state and\n   supplied by the composition that wires the VM.\n\n     (-> (empty-registry)\n         (register-module 'my.lib {'foo (fn [x] x)})\n         (register-stream-module))\n\n   Nothing in this namespace runs at load time, and nothing registers itself.\n   A composition that wants Yin source to reach `(stream/...)` registers the\n   stream module explicitly and supplies `:make-stream` beside it.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.string :as str]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Registry values\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn empty-registry\n  \"An empty module registry value.\"\n  []\n  {:modules {}, :effect-handlers {}})\n\n\n(defn- symbol->path\n  \"Split a dotted symbol into a get-in path of symbols.\n   'yin.io.file-input-stream → ['yin 'io 'file-input-stream]\"\n  [sym]\n  (mapv symbol (str/split (str sym) #\"\\.\")))\n\n\n(defn register-module\n  \"Return registry with bindings merged into module-name.\n   module-name is a dotted symbol; bindings is a map of symbol -> function.\"\n  [registry module-name bindings]\n  (update-in (or registry (empty-registry))\n             (into [:modules] (symbol->path module-name))\n             merge\n             bindings))\n\n\n(defn resolve-module\n  \"Get a value from a registry value by dotted symbol path.\n   (resolve-module r 'io)                    → full 'io bindings map\n   (resolve-module r 'io.file-output-stream) → the specific binding\"\n  [registry sym]\n  (get-in (:modules registry) (symbol->path sym)))\n\n\n(defn register-effect-handler\n  \"Return registry with handler-fn installed for effect keyword kw.\n   Handler: (fn [state effect opts] -> {:state s :value v :blocked? bool})\"\n  [registry kw handler-fn]\n  (assoc-in (or registry (empty-registry)) [:effect-handlers kw] handler-fn))\n\n\n(defn get-effect-handler\n  \"Get the handler function for an effect keyword.\"\n  [registry kw]\n  (get (:effect-handlers registry) kw))\n\n\n(defn list-modules\n  \"List the top-level module names in a registry value.\"\n  [registry]\n  (keys (:modules registry)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Effect descriptors\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn effect?\n  \"Check if a value is an effect descriptor.\"\n  [x]\n  (and (map? x) (contains? x :effect)))\n\n\n(defn make-effect\n  \"Create an effect descriptor.\"\n  [effect-type & {:as params}]\n  (assoc params :effect effect-type))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The `stream` module\n;; =============================================================================\n;;\n;; These are pure data constructors. They touch no stream: Yin source calls\n;; them through the module system, they return effect maps, and the engine\n;; interprets those. `take!` is deliberately absent — destructive read is one\n;; reader's progress and every other reader's data loss, and a v2 `take!` would\n;; need the reader-position-in-the-medium the contract retired.\n\n(defn make\n  ([] (make nil))\n  ([cap] {:effect :stream/make, :capacity cap}))\n\n\n(defn put!\n  [s v]\n  {:effect :stream/put, :stream s, :val v})\n\n\n(defn cursor\n  [s]\n  {:effect :stream/cursor, :stream s})\n\n\n(defn next!\n  [c]\n  {:effect :stream/next, :cursor c})\n\n\n(defn close!\n  [s]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
